ilosophy to be in some respect or  in some manners aided by what we would  typically associate with religion so  let's take a look at what he actually  says the fundamental question is how do  we know about the existence and the  nature that is what God is like of the  divine right and he says it's a great  and very rare thing for a person after  contemplating the whole creation  corporeal and incorporeal to  ass beyond those and to rise to  something higher than that now notice  that he says that this involves not just  looking at the visible or bodily  corporeal aspect of the created world or  even if you don't want to accept it as a  Korean world to say the universe right  simply studying physics or neuroscience  or biology or whatever it is that you  want to study alone would not get you to  this point you also have to study the  incorporeal and to you know because it's  incorporeal invisible world as well so  you would have to engage say in  introspective psychology which is  something that Augustine himself does  now through doing this through passing  beyond the created order one can reach  as he says the substance of God and when  you do that you do that through  contemplation through that is the use of  our faculties that we've been given  reason intellect however else you want  to understand it and now notice what he  says in this case this is not merely a  grasping on our part as the agent of a  passive object it becomes a relationship  it becomes a dialogue he talks about he  says learning from God himself that none  but he has made all that is not of the  divine essence and then he's got a  little bit of reflection here about how  God would speak to human beings and  that's worth looking at he says God  speaks to a person not by means of some  audible sounds didn't in their ears with  atmospheric vibrations or anything like  that he says not by these but by the  truth itself so in our intellectual  grasp of truth using our faculties that  are part of you know our endowment as  creatures it's part of a creator  order were able to engage with the truth  itself which is not a passive thing or  relation say between thought and and  reality but is the the reality itself  revealing itself in an active way to the  person so he says he speaks to that part  of the human being which is better than  all else in the human being and that  then which God himself alone is better  so he talks about this as well as how  were created in the divine image which  is something that you find in the book  of Genesis and then you know mentioned  at many other points in the canonical  scriptures that Augustine is going to be  talking about this notion of being  created in the divine image is not  unique to Judaism and Christianity  you'll find the Stoics also referencing  something like this a part of you know  ourselves  the highest part reason the rational  part is a part of God and alike to God  but this is some very important idea and  so Augustine says that's perfectly fine  that's open that's a possibility  philosophers or people who want to  engage in philosophy can in fact if  they're conducting their minds properly  attain to God without any sort of  scriptures and he sort of priesthood any  sort of rituals it's perfectly possible  however well that's true you might say  in the abstract for most people it's not  true or it's not possible rather in the  concrete why because we're damaged  screwed-up human beings and this  rational or intellectual part of  ourselves has been darkened it's been  miss shapen it's been led astray and he  attributes this in in large part to what  we can call the vices to all sorts of  things like pride or greed or law  and we could go on and on and on talking  about examples of that but it's not  really necessary his point here as he  says is this higher part the reason and  intelligence is disabled and it's  disabled from what not only from  enjoying the divine which is what you  would attain if you know you were having  a clearer sight according to Augustine  of the divine but from even fully  understanding it so for many people this  approach is at least for the moment  closed off although it's not closed off  for everybody and I've seen as offering  the possibility that some people and we  can call them philosophers if we like  we're able to do this so what can we do  in that case this is why he brings up  the notion of a mediator an intermediary  and notice how the mediation is taking  place it's not simply like a you know  game of telephone where you hear what  the one person has to say and then you  say the same thing down the line of  course the message gets garbled along  the way or anything like that instead  it's about a relation something that is  actually ontological something that  changes the fabric of the created order  as well as you know representing a new  way of understanding the divine so he  tells us that truth itself God or God's  Son and for Augustine is a Trinitarian  Christian these are of course going to  be the same in this man Jesus mediates  as he says between God and human beings  how does this happen  so he's got a very interesting and short  discussion of this he says he takes on  humanity without thereby losing his  divinity so he becomes as you know in  traditional Trinitarian theology he  becomes the god man  or if we want to avoid the sort of  gender exclusive there you say God human  he becomes Thais anthropos right he  becomes this bridge and Augustine has  this really nice sentence in here which  I have depicted by drawing this the sort  of arrow between human and God the human  being  is aiming at God as his or her end as  their goal and there's a lot of  different ways this could be parsed out  happiness knowledge wisdom all of those  sorts of things a renewal of themselves  moving away from vice to viciousness  however we want to frame it the human  being is aiming at God what allows there  to be this connection between the human  being and God is the God human which  uses the human as a means to reach the  end of God in this case the human in  question is Jesus Jesus is being used as  they means as a mediator as the thing in  between to attain that end and here you  know you might think about means and  ends brings to mind that does the end  justify the means well yes in this case  completely and justify in more than one  way but it is also the means  that's justifying the human being in a  different sense making them right making  them able to reach God the end that they  have in mind now Augustine goes on to  talk about revelation in different forms  so he says that this this mediator  reveals the truth about God first  through the prophets and then through  Jesus himself and what it is that he has  to say recorded you know in the things  that his followers write down but also  presumably and how they behaved and  interacted the things that they didn't  write down but remembered and put into  play and then through these apostles  people who are going or  and preaching the good news and  eventually through the Scriptures being  canonized being brought into a Canon or  a rule and a rule not just for this is  what counts as is you know  official stuff but a rule for practice a  rule for what it is that they would do  so for example there's a book that it is  in the Canon for you know everybody from  Catholics on eastward including the  Orthodox and the Oriental Orthodox  churches called you know Sioux rock and  that's a book of wisdom it's also called  the queasy asticus meaning the church  book because it was used as a rule for  practice very much you know set of  philosophical discussions in their  practical philosophy and that would  allow the human being to understand God  right so the last thing that he talks  about is the need for some sort of  testimony for some sort of informing  process or witnessing and he says the  things that were being given in order to  help us reach God are the things that we  need to know but can't figure out on our  own and you might say well you know when  I read scripture there's a lot of things  in there that are pretty common-sense  well might not be that you need to know  that thing could be that somebody else  needs that as a reminder right it's not  necessarily all tailored exactly at one  individual but it's it's supposed to be  you know providing a basis that could be  used for understanding that so Augustine  will go on and he'll make a sort of a  fortiori argument that is an argument  saying hey if you're gonna say that this  is you know valid in this respect then  it's even more valid in this respect he  talks about things in the world things  that are visible things that are  corporeal things that we can touch and  smell and taste and hear and he says  there's a lot of them we can learn from  our own experience right I mean  if I'm wearing clothes or not I don't  need to ask somebody else unless I'm you  know blinder in some sort of really  strange condition I can look down yep  dressed today not in that dream where I  don't have any pants or anything like  that right but there's a lot of things  where we routinely rely on the testimony  of others why because it's natural we  can't see the whole world we allow them  to tell us things and you know sometimes  they lie to us or but they're  misinformed or you know we don't fully  trust them but in many cases we do and  we couldn't actually get by in the world  if we didn't so he says a little bit  later accordingly as in the case of  visible objects which we've not seen we  trust those who have so in the case of  things which are perceived by the mind  and spirit which are remote from our own  interior sense it behooves us to trust  those who have seen them set in that  incorporeal light or abiding ly  contemplate them now what is the  implication of that how does that affect  all these other points that we've been  talking about so there's really you know  two ways to get at the truth about God  one is through our own contemplation of  God and if we can do that great we might  also rely on other people who are able  to do that sort of thing and perhaps  they can give us some useful guidance or  information about that or we might go to  the mediator who is there specifically  for that role and provides some way of  making it past our own screwed-up  intellect so that we can properly  understand these matters that have to do  with the incorporeal but incredibly  important at least in this perspective  divine so for Augustine this is the  account of why and how we need and rely  upon or use a mediator to get to God  you
 

